# DESIGN.md's threading model; docs/background-work-plan.md.
#
# THE WORKER PARKS UNTIL A CLICK RELEASES IT, and only a live app thread
# can process a click, so a binding that let background work occupy the
# app thread fails every assertion below the park at once. It does NOT
# hang (every expect is a bounded retry), so THE CASCADE is the
# signature: merely slow fails one assertion, wedged fails all of them.
expect label#0 "idle"
click button#0
expect label#0 "working"

# THE CLAIM: the worker is parked and the app thread must still serve
# input, so a binding that blocked dies here instead of later.
click button#1
expect label#1 "alive"

# The posts have NOT landed yet: a guest that computed eagerly on the
# app thread and only pretended to park shows the final value here.
expect label#0 "working"

# Three posts, accumulated, so this is a test of ORDER: a stack instead
# of a queue reads "321".
click button#2
expect label#0 "123"

# A post from INSIDE a transaction QUEUES for after and never nests: the
# handler appends a, posts a closure appending b, appends c, so it
# commits "ac" and the closure then "acb". Nesting could only ever
# produce "abc", and the two strings are unreachable from each other —
# thread identity is not observable from a scene, so this is the one
# discriminator available.
click button#3
expect label#2 "acb"

# One REAL-TREE read at the end: everything above reads kaya's own model
# and would pass for an arm that drew nothing.
expect_ax label#2 "label/acb"
